From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 23:26:55 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 4/9] Electrum protocol 1.8 on a chain with v2 headers, and a
 backend chain check

On a chain whose tip is a v2 header (164 bytes, BLAKE2b proof of work),
Frigate now follows the rule Electrs Pruned uses (its patch 0006) and
docs/electrum-header-v2.md in the electrs-pruned repository:

- it negotiates protocol 1.8 only with wallets, and refuses a lower
  version with a reason. A client below 1.8 would read 80 bytes of a
  164-byte header and fail to link the chain without saying why;
- the rule is also checked where Frigate serves headers itself
  (headers.subscribe, block.header, block.headers), and a subscribed
  client that negotiated lower is disconnected when the tip turns v2,
  rather than sent a header it would misread;
- the backend is asked for a range reaching 1.8 whatever the wallet's
  own maximum, since Electrs Pruned refuses anything lower on this chain.
  A backend answering lower (privkeyio's Fulcrum stops at 1.6) still
  serves the chain's headers, in one of the two reply forms a 1.8 client
  must read, so the wallet is told 1.8 either way;
- server.features reports 1.8 as Frigate's own range and carries
  blake2b_fork, passed through from the backend (the record used to drop
  unknown fields) or found on the node by binary search when the backend
  does not report it. The Sparrow BLAKE2b fork cross-checks it.

On every other chain negotiation is unchanged.

The backend chain check compares one recent header byte for byte, the
backend's blockchain.block.header against the node's raw header at the
same height, at startup and every ten minutes on a connection of its
own. Two chains sharing history differ in every header above their split.
A backend on another chain refuses wallets at server.version with a
reason, and the log says FAILED; one that could not be checked is not
held against them.
